It would. I bodged up a quick hack to the source that does the following:

Increments "moves" whenever a scroll of enchant weapon is generated.

Added a loop at the start of moveloop() in allmain.c that moves you down a
level as long as Can_dig_down(&u.uz) is true - thus immediately punting
you down to the Castle. And then kills you immediately. But what about the
Mines and Sokoban? Sigh. 

Insert a return; into vpline() in pline.c, suppressing all messages.

Invoke nethack with -@ suppressing the role selection prompts, setting
!legacy in .nethackrc. Also set disclose in .nethackrc to suppress all
prompts.

Suppress the calls to topten() in end.c. Jigger it so it prints the number
of moves directly and doesn't print any of the usual post-game stuff. Now
we've got a fiddled-about nethack that effectively prints the number of EW
scrolls generated and exits.

Wrap the whole thing in:

perl -we 'while (1) { $foo=`~/games/nethack -@ |tail -n 1`; print $foo; }'

Just as a sanity check, remove the increment of "moves" in mkobj.c. Run
that. It faithfully sits there printing "1 move" over and over, so there
isn't something else that increments "moves" in these games. Gosh, a
sensible person would have added u.scrlcnt rather than taken such a
stupid shortcut. Never mind.

Assessing death drops is harder. Did you know you only get death drops
when a monster is killed by you? And death drop items are created first
and then delobj()ed if the monster is an inappropriate size, which
thankfully doesn't affect scrolls.

Running this as-is, about 10% of dungeons didn't contain any EW scrolls.
But... ignoring death drops, the Mines, and Sokoban, is a significant
error. I guess I might tinker with it further if anyone's interested.
